1
00:00:00,267 --> 00:00:10,611
This video shows how to put an animation from Pixodesk SVG Animator into a React app. It takes one package, one file and one component.

2
00:00:11,833 --> 00:00:13,949
Let's start with the app as it is.

3
00:00:18,067 --> 00:00:20,496
A heading, and nothing that moves yet.

4
00:00:21,733 --> 00:00:27,115
To play animations, the app needs the Pixodesk player. Let's install it.

5
00:00:33,400 --> 00:00:35,699
The React player is in, one package.

6
00:00:36,933 --> 00:00:42,785
Now for the animation itself. Let's switch to the editor, where a football is already drawn.

7
00:00:44,333 --> 00:00:46,763
It's a vector drawing, standing still.

8
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:50,691
To make it move, let's switch on Auto mode.

9
00:00:53,200 --> 00:00:56,936
With Auto mode on, every change becomes a keyframe.

10
00:00:58,167 --> 00:01:01,458
Half a second in, let's drag the ball down to the floor.

11
00:01:04,567 --> 00:01:08,302
A keyframe appears on the timeline, holding the new position.

12
00:01:09,533 --> 00:01:11,649
At one second, let's bring it back up.

13
00:01:14,933 --> 00:01:18,799
Now it falls and rises again, in one second.

14
00:01:20,033 --> 00:01:26,564
A real ball speeds up as it falls, and slows down as it rises. Let's set that easing.

15
00:01:33,700 --> 00:01:38,245
Gravity easing, falling and rising, makes the bounce feel natural.

16
00:01:39,467 --> 00:01:42,654
One bounce is not enough, so let's loop the animation.

17
00:01:46,133 --> 00:01:50,313
Loop is saved with the file, so the animation repeats forever.

18
00:01:52,500 --> 00:01:57,646
The animation is ready. Let's save it as JSON, straight into the project.

19
00:02:02,600 --> 00:02:04,298
Now, back to our app.

20
00:02:05,833 --> 00:02:09,125
The animation file is already there, next to the code.

21
00:02:10,367 --> 00:02:13,919
Let's import the player, and the animation file.

22
00:02:21,800 --> 00:02:26,502
The component comes from the package, the animation from the JSON file.

23
00:02:27,733 --> 00:02:30,424
Then, let's place the player on the page.

24
00:02:39,833 --> 00:02:49,316
The doc property takes the animation. Autoplay means it plays as the file says, starting on load and repeating forever.

25
00:02:50,533 --> 00:02:53,015
Let's reload the page, and watch.

26
00:02:58,133 --> 00:03:01,999
There it is. The ball bounces, right inside the React app.

27
00:03:04,033 --> 00:03:13,333
So, one package, one file saved from the editor, and one component. That's all it takes to bring an animation into a React app.
